#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "7d93dd10-5319-58e7-9106-f8c675dc9e65" :question/math-academy-id "e-12567" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119437 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "42109de0-eb59-53f9-982e-1433f630b067" :question/math-academy-id "e-12568" :question/problem "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "26c3579b-5b24-5e84-9024-5b2597856d20" :question/math-academy-id "e-12569" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "e8c24f26-7658-5251-922a-4b34043a71d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12580" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 6t+6 & =0 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}+1\\,,\\,6(-1)+3⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75dfcf67-6ac6-55be-8a71-4b622b2bfabf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327990} {:answer/id #uuid "24f81c52-2c82-593e-a0a7-b48540054490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327991} {:answer/id #uuid "1bba1d31-29e6-57f9-abe8-8ebbda57a616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327992} {:answer/id #uuid "babf67fb-e170-5e20-9eaf-54b7245fd7e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327993} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b80ae2-41ee-5d70-90ed-ee8fe5e79819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i+j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bba1d31-29e6-57f9-abe8-8ebbda57a616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327992} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3bc720e-6d96-58eb-afc5-b1b998b10361" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b20167b-4318-505b-9cb5-6c2420c65eed" :question/math-academy-id "q-126738" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=(1-t)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(2,-1,-1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-j-k \\\\ (1-t)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k & =2\\,i-j-k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-t=2 \\\\ -{t}^{2}=-1 \\\\ 2t+1=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-1,-1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(-1).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i-2t\\,j+2\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(-1) & =-i-2(-1)\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-1,-1)$ is $-i+2\\,j+2\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deb6fb4f-5339-5b74-9add-c3e30549f18c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-8\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327997} {:answer/id #uuid "0192fd77-cb66-5e5a-b5ed-0c1404e58d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327998} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c437dc-97eb-5a5a-a08b-e5fd4a6aa48f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327999} {:answer/id #uuid "9bda32d7-5faa-50e9-ba02-91217dfb9842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328000} {:answer/id #uuid "76def20f-207d-5d53-8045-fe6bb5fbcc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i+6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76def20f-207d-5d53-8045-fe6bb5fbcc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i+6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328001} :answer-field/id #uuid "f384411b-3df5-543a-ac7f-98e30d61a666" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0ecbb89-df3b-51f8-acb9-98f79c6ce59a" :question/math-academy-id "q-126739" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=(3t+2)\\,i+(6t+1)\\,j+(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k$ at the point $P(2,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+j+k \\\\ (3t+2)\\,i+(6t+1)\\,j+(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k & =2\\,i+j+k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3t+2=2 \\\\ 6t+1=1 \\\\ 1-4{t}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j-8t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =3\\,i+6\\,j-8(0)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,1,1)$ is $3\\,i+6\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da597414-513f-5bd6-a970-4468bc79f0b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328004} {:answer/id #uuid "2c757345-f3f3-5125-ab6e-f2e00fbbcf5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328005} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8bd160-4b5d-5a9b-a2fe-be7939a0f27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328006} {:answer/id #uuid "930d540f-d415-5223-b285-f6ced5ef39a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328007} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9091cf-5dfd-5a21-9a8d-08e15352a916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da597414-513f-5bd6-a970-4468bc79f0b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328004} :answer-field/id #uuid "d04997be-fe9b-53ad-ae93-7a7906d1c3d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b063a87b-7453-5eed-82c3-d4c7e3cbe51f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126838" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi ,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\pi )+u{r}^{′}(\\pi ),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7154d93d-de75-56a1-bacc-660fc5ac68e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{11}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328011} {:answer/id #uuid "997c2c9f-756d-5aad-9cf3-5fc273173c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{11}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328012} {:answer/id #uuid "2eab77d1-18f6-5cc3-afe3-61552cd5c4d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328013} {:answer/id #uuid "3e67894e-98dc-5e96-9ad6-4868cf7275ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{2},\\frac{5}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328014} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d92310-afeb-55d0-9fcd-f446898824f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{2},\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eab77d1-18f6-5cc3-afe3-61552cd5c4d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328013} :answer-field/id #uuid "3103a0e1-125a-52fd-9784-1228b130125a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "042122be-55d5-57f3-8f68-09bada51b89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126896" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨1-{t}^{2},3t+4⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $⟨1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-2t,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1(-2t)+1(3) & =0 \\\\ -2t+3 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨1-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},3(\\frac{3}{2})+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\frac{9}{4},\\frac{9}{2}+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "699a823e-4838-5137-b03d-f6a732e715e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,1)$ and $(\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{5}{9})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328018} {:answer/id #uuid "b32c7574-cb0d-573d-9017-1f128c77b33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{5}{9})$  and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328019} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0ce1bd-503f-5b8f-8c9d-d6dfc31b5599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328020} {:answer/id #uuid "582b6dea-a368-54ef-b9d0-c40d1ec45077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,-1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328021} {:answer/id #uuid "83f643ab-d810-55b4-9966-922d8f175d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f643ab-d810-55b4-9966-922d8f175d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328022} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f05c771-1e23-5860-984c-a30174630aae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f4d84f3-3a15-51fb-b461-d9499204e5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-126901" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨4t-{t}^{2},{t}^{3}⟩,$ find all points at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨2,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨2,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =c⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨2c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4-2t=2c \\\\ 3{t}^{2}=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2-t.$ Substituting in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & =2-t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+t-2 & =0 \\\\ (3t-2)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨2,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=-1,\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨4(-1)-(-1)^{2},(-1)^{3}⟩=⟨-5,-1⟩ \\\\ r(\\frac{2}{3}) & =⟨4(\\frac{2}{3})-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2},{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}⟩=⟨\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨2,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "386a8e88-de32-5831-bdb5-85795e1d2399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328025} {:answer/id #uuid "c89c6f95-a8f3-54b9-ab1d-8be1f7e0c964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(64,2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328026} {:answer/id #uuid "7f33d3ac-9be7-544d-9727-9c66e3d42056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328027} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a9126f-b14f-5914-a8c9-80961513fd48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(64,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328028} {:answer/id #uuid "b288b864-0023-5335-a0cc-a218f9362611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\,,\\,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b288b864-0023-5335-a0cc-a218f9362611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\,,\\,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328029} :answer-field/id #uuid "d449187f-9d2a-52f8-acf8-1d353263baac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "defbfa0d-289a-592b-ade4-ad5a241bdd53" :question/math-academy-id "q-164833" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{5}\\,,\\,{t}^{2}-2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{5})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 10{t}^{4}+(-5)\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 10{t}^{4}-10t & =0 \\\\ 10t({t}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{5}\\,,\\,(1)^{2}-2⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2\\,,\\,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d5b56d-6552-52d7-9efb-e3714e0d87d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,8)$ and $(9,12)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328032} {:answer/id #uuid "bd364d65-4c0c-51f2-9632-6a0645d558ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328033} {:answer/id #uuid "79f84984-4398-5809-a8e2-8b519698f838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-8,0)$ and $(-7,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328034} {:answer/id #uuid "f82592c4-c7d9-5ef7-8a1f-1c78c6833673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,-4)$ and $(-4,-8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328035} {:answer/id #uuid "fccbd3dc-b4f7-5913-96ab-187246987439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fccbd3dc-b4f7-5913-96ab-187246987439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328036} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab44c741-1a24-52ea-9fae-b1a07f899a09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d74d4cc0-e4d2-548e-a475-bf349405cf50" :question/math-academy-id "q-164836" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-8)\\,i-4t\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-8)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [({t}^{2}-8)\\,i-4t\\,j]\\cdot (2t\\,i-4\\,j) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-8)\\cdot 2t+(-4t)\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{3}-16t+16t & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=({0}^{2}-8)\\,i-4(0)\\,j=-8i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(-8,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5ee30d3-cf82-524d-871b-5023d0f8b770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328039} {:answer/id #uuid "27973700-0ec0-53e3-95f5-7390ff00971b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,-4)$ and $(24,26)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328040} {:answer/id #uuid "d4350778-93a1-58da-bf62-dc81cb1cb685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-9,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328041} {:answer/id #uuid "25b0bb99-d492-5ef8-9d92-5a6b5a421ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-9,-4)$ and $(-8,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328042} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0ddfff-895f-509b-a32b-59b04aa7250f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d0ddfff-895f-509b-a32b-59b04aa7250f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328043} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae510853-2865-50da-b394-02843360d1fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "174f7b2b-5f48-524a-983a-b81649b2d307" :question/math-academy-id "q-165038" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{5}-8,2{t}^{4}-6⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}-8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =⟨c,\\frac{4c}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{4}=c \\\\ 8{t}^{3}=\\frac{4c}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=5{t}^{4}.$ Substituting $c=5{t}^{4}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{t}^{3} & =\\frac{4(5{t}^{4})}{5} \\\\ 8{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}(2-t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=2.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨(2)^{5}-8,2(2)^{4}-6⟩=⟨24,26⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(24,26).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022635c3-365e-5ba4-a622-5920d444af77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328046} {:answer/id #uuid "99163740-bc18-5635-82e0-62e10dd759e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(e,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $({e}^{-2},\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328047} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbe3d5d-1a19-5ebb-abf7-82056512a1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328048} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf53f94-0d92-566e-84df-1651262caefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328049} {:answer/id #uuid "3b4ad1d7-ed77-5726-b826-8aa9a21071b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cf53f94-0d92-566e-84df-1651262caefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328049} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cd84109-53ed-50bc-9c91-d849ab549cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b31a7b97-1eea-55d0-bd37-511197dc3f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-165042" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =c(2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =2c{e}^{2t}\\,i+c(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}=2c{e}^{2t} \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2}=c(2t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting $c=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2t+1) \\\\ {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the point corresponding to $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & ={e}^{0}\\,i+({0}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90394e14-ffcd-539a-8653-76848dc40102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328053} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed64811-b657-538c-a5c0-793518a752c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328054} {:answer/id #uuid "de101b42-fe1c-5a02-9aee-8ab97f3c9005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5,2)$ and $(\\sqrt{3},32,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328055} {:answer/id #uuid "04126353-fc2f-5d38-8da0-af0d626fe233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,6,4)$ and $(\\sqrt{2},13,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328056} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fcd87f-a80f-5aa7-a37b-76d18c146d44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},13,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ed64811-b657-538c-a5c0-793518a752c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328054} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d426ef4-94f8-5cb6-ba02-bf5da2cf2a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "67ae79a9-8de8-5def-9b50-77f6347f01af" :question/math-academy-id "q-165046" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t}\\,,\\,{t}^{3}+5\\,,\\,2t+2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,{t}^{3}+5,\\,2t+2⟩ & =c\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,{t}^{3}+5,\\,2t+2⟩ & =⟨\\frac{c}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3c{t}^{2},\\,2c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{t}=\\frac{c}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ {t}^{3}+5=3c{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t+2=2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2t.$ Substituting into the third equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+2 & =2\\cdot (2t) \\\\ 2t+2 & =4t \\\\ 2t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis satisfies also the second equation ${t}^{3}+5=3c{t}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$ These values also satisfy the second equation. \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\sqrt{1}\\,,\\,{1}^{3}+5\\,,\\,2(1)+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,6,\\,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,6,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c43b6daf-aa85-5bba-98c1-47b6a22b594a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 2, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117391} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d88588-ef76-5b00-bb1d-b137c461ad7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4, 4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117392} {:answer/id #uuid "de9a1398-aed1-5a44-8ab3-a2e31fb96c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117393} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5b6cab-7226-55ef-bdaf-b517cc71e5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117394} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0ab2de-0954-547c-930d-34fd15fd5a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b5b6cab-7226-55ef-bdaf-b517cc71e5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117394} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa839e10-db01-577e-91c1-f151d47846a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e23a960-2958-5b70-bc1c-e4672cc3a4bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-32916" :question/problem "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\pi $,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)],\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)]⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-(-1)),\\,2(1+0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $  is  $⟨4,\\,2⟩$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4842bc55-3241-5fdb-bea9-8e8c770f5aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+3\\,j-3\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328060} {:answer/id #uuid "47fb00a9-cee0-5cc9-90a8-109ef0148970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328061} {:answer/id #uuid "8d892452-e4d4-53fa-b7c0-ccb983acd1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i-6\\,j-3\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328062} {:answer/id #uuid "81d1c8fd-8ace-5a3f-9061-989b0e92f37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(i+6\\,j+6\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328063} {:answer/id #uuid "87b4db26-4f4c-5ad2-921f-f7ea921ebf02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47fb00a9-cee0-5cc9-90a8-109ef0148970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328061} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6e8dba0-9afc-5189-b1a7-2f8df6214fdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "628cfa4c-a200-5f63-920a-e20b0b14eb94" :question/math-academy-id "q-32920" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve  $r(t)=\\sqrt{1+t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+(3t-1)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,4,8),$  valid for all real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{0}$ corresponds to the value of $t$ at the point $P.$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k \\\\ \\sqrt{1+t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+(3t-1)\\,k & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+t}=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-5=4 \\\\ 3t-1=8\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=3.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(3).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1+t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1+t}}\\,i+2t\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1+(3)}}\\,i+2(3)\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,i+2\\cdot 3\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(2,4,8)$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e538a501-64f6-55ea-8389-d0e60f3efe27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328067} {:answer/id #uuid "788f99a1-e02a-5740-96e9-e431b9a0caa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328068} {:answer/id #uuid "fefb3c5c-ad2f-5a36-9b2e-f9cb6d12fa5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+2\\sqrt{5}\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328069} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea4a109-0cb1-5260-9aa8-2c52133d5bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328070} {:answer/id #uuid "df3ce20d-2036-5942-8747-5bfefb0e995c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "788f99a1-e02a-5740-96e9-e431b9a0caa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328068} :answer-field/id #uuid "db02f2ab-6293-50e2-9724-6d3d85cbd317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c191617d-e728-5261-9424-d2d7dff0d6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-33457" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j+(t-1)\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=2,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=2$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(2)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r(2)+u{r}^{′}(2),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $r(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =({2}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}}\\,j+(2-1)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(2) & =2(2)\\,i+\\frac{(2)}{\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}}}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=2$ is \n\n$$\nR(u)=3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "316ae081-928b-5565-9dbf-30b4dcd09ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(-\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328074} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f107f4-57b7-5477-90f2-8b1c92eab0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(2\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328075} {:answer/id #uuid "0b309581-2f7e-5146-968b-f0aed58c91e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(i-\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328076} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b459bd-b7e0-5d77-a96e-5b6e03a657fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i+2\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328077} {:answer/id #uuid "738fc50c-2493-53cb-b0ff-e857dba80d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "738fc50c-2493-53cb-b0ff-e857dba80d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328078} :answer-field/id #uuid "21f12857-a212-588b-8b4b-db5fc9d02ad5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2da0d02a-cfac-5869-8101-5b8d71d9f0d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-33461" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{sin}^{2}t\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+u{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j+{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j-\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})k \\\\  & =\\,i-\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f8ba848-6dc3-5228-a3f7-7f748621909b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328081} {:answer/id #uuid "4161c3a2-5b19-5629-9d06-762a0f213c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328082} {:answer/id #uuid "487745d1-8e9f-5cd1-b1c9-c671d2d6cfb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328083} {:answer/id #uuid "bd702f66-25b5-5153-af76-0c68dc6f472d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328084} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4392eb-f545-5f1d-99dd-d2da1653d1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "487745d1-8e9f-5cd1-b1c9-c671d2d6cfb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328083} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8906b4a-d842-52d0-b715-62d111590a9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53f0b906-751c-5a39-a9c0-60bcb90cd863" :question/math-academy-id "q-33491" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+3t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\pi ,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\pi $,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\pi )+u{r}^{′}(\\pi ),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & ={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3(\\pi )\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+3\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{(\\pi )}\\,i+3\\,j+\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2(\\pi )k \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\pi $ is \n\n$$\nR(u)={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aef6dafa-2e60-5e9b-a177-67ae78d626e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1)$ and $(2, 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117637} {:answer/id #uuid "0444b013-203c-597d-8d1a-207ab2748493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117638} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc1336b-4154-589f-aeb7-536301c7858a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1)$, $(2, 2)$, and $(-2, 2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117639} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4ca137-a16c-53c6-bff5-cb55721644c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2, 3)$ and $(-2, 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117640} {:answer/id #uuid "20a58f35-b575-517b-99e2-921560f0176f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20a58f35-b575-517b-99e2-921560f0176f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117641} :answer-field/id #uuid "045680ab-260e-513f-9d4c-2dcae7ac41b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e8969d65-ef55-525e-9adb-a40d8a396669" :question/math-academy-id "q-33498" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j]\\cdot (i+t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\cdot t & =0 \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(4+{t}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0$ since $4+{t}^{2}>0$ for any $t.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=0\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})\\,j=j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4092311-d1b7-5fa7-b81f-0952d37a7097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3,2)$ and $(-1,3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328088} {:answer/id #uuid "b024fe5f-5064-5c4d-84f4-28d34e27453f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,10,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328089} {:answer/id #uuid "3a1c4f15-f41d-5503-920d-a11c8eba1348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328090} {:answer/id #uuid "90d85cb5-751b-5d46-8738-f5b4f57ae23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328091} {:answer/id #uuid "99f37592-7eba-5993-9be1-2bc6083a52ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,1)$ and $(-1,1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90d85cb5-751b-5d46-8738-f5b4f57ae23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328091} :answer-field/id #uuid "206d5385-55fb-55ed-93f0-a1927392cc00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd4dff7b-a812-5fe4-af3d-a9ec36938cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-33509" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ t\\,i+(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k & =c(i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}t=c \\\\ 2+{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ 1+{t}^{2}=c(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $t=c.$ Substituting $c=t$ into the second equality gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{3} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =1 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis satisfies also the equation $1+{t}^{2}=c(2t).$\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =i+(2+{1}^{3})\\,j+(1+{1}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+2\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $P(1,3,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d240ba9e-2fd9-5462-8d88-96f96a61ba91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328095} {:answer/id #uuid "f3870c7a-22b3-54a0-917c-0829eef5ce87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328096} {:answer/id #uuid "46f83cbc-f3be-5c80-b3a2-24179c3cd4e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328097} {:answer/id #uuid "7badfff3-9e9b-53b6-92bf-6b50d51a7f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328098} {:answer/id #uuid "80db05c6-bce8-5ffa-80e6-b6e77df4cdb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80db05c6-bce8-5ffa-80e6-b6e77df4cdb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328099} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6396df1-1582-59bb-8966-ab67747b03a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4e2126e-1bb5-51e9-8af9-c9e954a25966" :question/math-academy-id "q-75197" :question/problem "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t+\\frac{1}{t},\\,t-\\frac{1}{t}⟩$ at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=1$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(1)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}},\\,1+\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-1,\\,1+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=1$  is  $⟨0,\\,2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ccae40a-c05e-5108-b155-5fff8cfc24cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328102} {:answer/id #uuid "82484957-e575-5917-b084-ebe13707908c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328103} {:answer/id #uuid "7263bf22-9806-5b79-9415-26b8c6cbf1dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328104} {:answer/id #uuid "045ec626-4bb0-5155-9f5b-3cd212cfd554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328105} {:answer/id #uuid "e22efb53-c074-5d1d-90d6-5a91b01e98d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ccae40a-c05e-5108-b155-5fff8cfc24cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328102} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6fedb9a-0560-5929-9f74-772e9fc53871" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "526be92a-5b07-5046-b534-daad997a7509" :question/math-academy-id "q-75198" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,i+{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,i+{sec}^{2}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  is  $\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30a549bb-50f4-54aa-8266-852c96938604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328109} {:answer/id #uuid "a04e64ae-3187-5dfa-a3b3-dd29aa20e0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328110} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fdb71a-700f-54bb-beb9-f596bea88169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328111} {:answer/id #uuid "c027d295-4c8c-50db-a1b9-5102e43442bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328112} {:answer/id #uuid "05bde8ce-1bef-565e-ae86-92105df82556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a04e64ae-3187-5dfa-a3b3-dd29aa20e0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328110} :answer-field/id #uuid "274fcc18-8a69-50a5-8f1a-6355799adb44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b197f356-fef7-5971-a2e8-3906da8e773d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75521" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j]\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=\\pm 1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)=1\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j=\\,i+j, \\\\ r(-1)=(-1)\\,i+\\frac{1}{(-1)}\\,j=-\\,i-j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$"}]}
